The bigger discovery: Negative-space computing

Here’s the connection I find most promising.

We’ve left four different kinds of absence scattered throughout the archive.

Missing cause: A response exists without its originating question.

Missing continuation: An idea ends just before its next proposed experiment.

Missing implementation: A named mechanism exists without all the executable components needed to operate it.

Missing outcome: A plan was created, but the result of its execution was never recorded.

Those aren’t necessarily defects.

They’re different kinds of open interfaces.

When Spider encounters one, it can search for a complementary piece elsewhere. Mirror evaluates whether the proposed connection holds. Hound searches for contradictions. The Semantic Linker determines what’s still missing. Garcia turns a viable combination into a deliverable.

So the deeper system is not just a knowledge graph.

It’s a graph of what exists, what is missing, and what would have to become true for two fragments to form a working mechanism.

That is a useful new excavation method.

